{"t":1789172251,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"064c1595-9e75-425f-822b-c1fc19774443","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-11T19:01:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 111901\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n301 PM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nContinue to monitor for a few thunderstorms in Central VA this\nafternoon. Beyond that, the flood potential remains for Saturday\nafternoon to evening, but uncertainty remains regarding where \nthe heaviest rain will fall.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) A few Central Virginia thunderstorms today, then likely \n  widespread convection Saturday into Saturday night.\n\n- 2) Dry conditions expected early next week ahead of a midweek\n  cold front. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A few Central Virginia thunderstorms today, \nthen likely widespread convection Saturday into Saturday night.\n\nThe slow moving frontal boundary is now across Central VA, with a \nfew showers and thunderstorms noted along the I-64 corridor. SPC has \na Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) along the far southern CWA centered \nin Nelson County. This is where low-level convergence and better \ninstability could result in a strong storm or two. The time window \nfor impacts in our area are limited as the front continues to push \nsouth. Most of the convection pushes south of our area by around 4-\n5PM. After that, conditions should be mostly dry through this \nevening. Cloud cover has kept temps in the 70s for most of the area, \nand even those north of the frontal boundary are also in the mid to \nupper 70s. \n\nSurface high pressure over southern New England slides offshore \nSaturday causing southeasterly flow to develop/strengthen over the \nMid-Atlantic. The synoptic pattern gets messy into the weekend as an \nelongated trough stretches from the Deep South to Mid-Atlantic. The \nstalled boundary over southern VA lifts north as a warm front \nSaturday morning, bringing a possibly first round of showers to the \narea. As the boundary lifts north of the MD/PA border, warm sector \nshowers are possible at time Saturday afternoon, though the bulk of \nconvection is set to arrive toward the evening hours.\n\nNumerous embedded shortwaves lift across the Tennessee Valley toward \nthe southern Mid-Atlantic, helping produce ample lift and shear \nacross the region. A surge of tropical moisture brings PWATs to \naround 2\" Saturday afternoon into Saturday night. A wave of surface \nlow pressure may try to develop somewhere in lee of the Alleghenies \nSaturday evening as the strongest shortwave traverses the region. A \nsubtle jet streak develops ahead of the upper trough, and while not \noverly impressive it will be enough to aid in widespread showers and \nthunderstorm development. Locally heavy rain is likely in some \nareas, though the 12Z high res guidance does not point to any one \nspecific location. Later analysis on areas of low-level convergence \nthat set up will be key to determine where a flash flood threat \ncould develop. But for now, have not issued any Flood Watches due to \nuncertainty in location of heaviest rain. WPC has expanded the \nSlight Risk area for excessive rainfall to include the Alleghenies \nwhere soils are more saturated from rain over the past month. Will \ncontinue to monitor the situation as new mesoscale guidance arrives.\n\nA cold front associated with the upper trough finally swings through \nthe area Sunday morning to afternoon, bringing an end to any \nlingering showers. This front won't bring much of an airmass change \nas temps still reach the 80s and humidity lingers.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions expected early next week ahead \nof a midweek cold front. \n\nUpper-level ridging begins to build over the Southeast beginning \nMonday and into Tuesday as the cold front transitions offshore. \nHumidity & temperatures decrease in the wake of the cold front on \nMonday, with dewpoints ranging in the 50s and low 60s as mid-level \ndry air transitions overhead. Monday night might be the first \nfall night of widespread lows in the 50s.\n\nOverall, anticipate seasonal, dry conditions to persist over the \nfollowing days as this ridging continues to build into the midweek \nperiod. Beginning Wednesday, this ridging begins to transition into \nmore of a zonal flow overhead as a trough further north forms over \nQuebec and Nova Scotia. An associated cold front could begin to \ntrack across the East Coast sometime during this period, although \ncurrent guidance shows some spread in the timing of this frontal \npassage. This front could bring some showers and thunderstorms to \nthe region, but probability of severe weather remains low at this \ntime. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nA few thunderstorms are possible around CHO through late this \nafternoon. A front that is moving south of the area today is going \nto stall tonight, then lift north as a warm front Saturday. Expect \nsub-VFR conditions to overspread the area from south to north \nSaturday morning. There is uncertainty as to how far north the sub-\nVFR CIGs are, but likely impact most of the terminals. Widespread \nshowers and scattered thunderstorms are expected areawide Saturday \nafternoon into late Saturday evening. TEMPO groups are likely to be \nneeded, but are just beyond the current TAF period. \n\nA couple of reinforcing cold fronts move through the region Sunday \ninto Sunday night, with VFR conditions returning in WNW/NW winds.\n\nPrevailing VFR conditions are anticipated across terminals Monday \nthrough Wednesday. A few lingering showers could temporarily bring \nreduced conditions near KCHO on Monday, but otherwise conditions \nremain mostly clear throughout Tuesday & Wednesday. Northerly winds \nshift southeasterly by Tuesday night. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nA slow moving front continues to slide south through the waters. The \nbrief surge of winds earlier this morning has now abated, with \noverall north winds around 10 knots. The front stalls south of the \nwaters tonight, then lifts north as a warm front Saturday night. An \nincrease in winds over the waters Saturday could be near SCA levels. \nLate afternoon to late evening thunderstorms could lead to hazardous \nmarine conditions, with Special Marine Warnings possibly needed. A \nsecondary front moves through Sunday, then a stronger cold front on \nMonday. Wind gusts could increase to near marginal SCA thresholds\non Monday before diminishing throughout the day Tuesday. Winds stay\nbelow SCA criteria through Wednesday. Northeasterly winds shift\nsoutheasterly in the wake of a cold front by Tuesday night. \n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nStrengthening onshore flow Saturday will bring tide levels up across \nthe area. Some locations could reach Action Stage by Saturday night \nto early Sunday high tides. A cold front moves through Sunday, and \nagain Monday, which should bring an end to any coastal flooding \npotential.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...KRR/SRT\nAVIATION...KRR/SRT\nMARINE...KRR/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1789172234,"days":{"2026-09-11":{"hi":null,"lo":69,"pop":11,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-09-12":{"hi":85,"lo":71,"pop":78,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers then Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-13":{"hi":87,"lo":70,"pop":48,"short":"Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-14":{"hi":82,"lo":59,"pop":5,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-15":{"hi":78,"lo":62,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-16":{"hi":83,"lo":65,"pop":3,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-17":{"hi":88,"lo":66,"pop":34,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-18":{"hi":81,"lo":null,"pop":21,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1789171200,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,n